[Unit]
Description=Cleanup /tmp files older than 3 days

# 🛑 .why this unit is HARDENED, and it is the only one here that must be
#
#    it is the sole unit in this repo that runs AS ROOT over a directory an
#    UNPRIVILEGED user writes freely. `/tmp` is world-writable by design, and
#    on a grove it is shared between both seats (`term=seat`). so every input
#    to this `find` — the names, the tree shape, the mtimes (`touch -t` sets
#    any) — is chosen by whoever the box does not trust.
#
#    ⇒ that inverts how a cleanup job is normally read. the question is not
#      "does it delete the right files"; it is "what is the WORST a hostile
#      /tmp can make root delete", and the answer must be bounded by the
#      SYSTEM, not by the find expression.
#
# .the two bounds, and why each is needed
#
#    1. `-xdev` — a `find` with no `-xdev` descends into any filesystem
#       mounted below its start. an unprivileged user with a user namespace
#       can bind-mount a directory under a /tmp path they made, and root's
#       walk then follows it OUT of /tmp and deletes there. `-xdev` refuses
#       to leave /tmp's own filesystem, so the mount is a dead end.
#
#    2. `ProtectSystem=strict` + `ReadWritePaths=/tmp` — the belt to that
#       suspenders. even if a future edit drops `-xdev`, or a traversal this
#       block did not anticipate reaches outside, the KERNEL refuses the
#       unlink: every path but /tmp is mounted read-only for this unit. one
#       declaration bounds every deletion the unit can ever perform, which is
#       the class fix a find-expression tweak is not (`rule.require.solve-at-cause`).
#
#    `ProtectHome=yes` makes /home, /root, and /run/user invisible here — the
#    single highest-value target, removed rather than guarded. `NoNewPrivileges`
#    and the trimmed capability set leave the unit no path to regain what the
#    rest of this block takes away.
#
# ⚠️ .why NOT a `-user` clause
#    it looks like a tighter filter and buys no safety: a hostile seat's own
#    files are exactly the ones it may steer, so a per-user clause narrows the
#    set to what an attacker fully controls. the bound that matters is on WHERE
#    the unit may write, never on WHOSE files it considers.
#
# 🛑 .why `! -type s` — MTIME IS A MEANINGLESS LIVENESS SIGNAL FOR A SOCKET
#
#    every other bound above answers "what is the worst a hostile /tmp can make
#    root delete". this one answers a different question: what must this unit
#    never delete, however old it looks.
#
#    a unix socket's mtime is set once, when the process BINDS it, and never
#    advances with traffic. so a server that has been up and busy for a week has
#    a socket whose mtime reads a week old, and the age test cannot tell it from
#    an abandoned file. the heuristic is not merely imprecise here — it reads a
#    quantity that carries no information about the subject.
#
# 🛑 .the cost, and why it lands hardest on a grove
#    tmux binds at `/tmp/tmux-<uid>/default`, and A DUCT IS TMUX. once that
#    socket is unlinked the server keeps every pane alive — it holds the fd —
#    and no new client can ever reach it. so `git.grove.send`, `duct.read`, and
#    `duct.send` all fail against a box whose state is intact and unreachable,
#    which is worse than a clean kill: the repair is to fell the server and
#    rebuild the duct, and the pane history goes with it.
#
#    ⇒ and it takes the CONTROL CHANNEL of the box, so the tool a human would
#      reach for to diagnose it is the tool that just broke.
#
# .why a TYPE test and not a name list
#    `-path '/tmp/tmux-*'` would work and would be a hand-written list of one,
#    free to go stale the day another socket matters — X11, ICE, and a font
#    server all bind under /tmp too. the type test names the actual cause, so it
#    covers every socket that will ever live there (`rule.require.solve-at-cause`).
#
# ⚠️ .why NOT `-prune`
#    `-delete` turns on `-depth`, and `-prune` has no effect under `-depth`. a
#    prune here would read as a guard and be inert — the shape where a check is
#    present and absent at once.
#
# .why this is safe to ship without a grove
#    it strictly NARROWS what root deletes. it adds no path to the set, so it
#    carries no new destructive reach to prove.
#
# 🛑 .THE EXEMPTION STRANDS THE SOCKET'S DIRECTORY — and `SuccessExitStatus`
#    is why this unit does not report a permanent failure for it
#
#    `-delete` implies `-depth`, so find still descends into `/tmp/tmux-<uid>`
#    and calls `rmdir` on it once that DIRECTORY's own mtime passes 3 days. the
#    socket inside is now exempt and stays, so the rmdir fails with ENOTEMPTY.
#
#    ⇒ before `! -type s`, the socket was deleted first and the rmdir
#      succeeded. so the exemption is what makes this CERTAIN, on every box
#      that has run tmux for more than three days — it is not a prior
#      condition this edit merely inherited.
#
#    find continues past the error, so the sweep still completes and no file is
#    left uncleaned. what it changes is the unit's EXIT STATUS, and a
#    `oneshot` that exits non-zero enters `failed` — permanently, on a healthy
#    box, for a directory it is correct to keep.
#
# .why `SuccessExitStatus=1` and not a narrower find expression
#    a `\( ! -type s \) -a \( ! -type d -o -empty \)` would suppress the rmdir
#    and also stop the unit from ever clearing a genuinely empty stale dir —
#    it trades a false failure for a silent skip, which is the worse half.
#
#    ⚠️ find's exit 1 means "an error occurred", so this DOES accept a wider
#      class than the one rmdir. that is the cost, and it is bounded by the
#      block above: `ProtectSystem=strict` + `ReadWritePaths=/tmp` mean the
#      widest error this can now mask is a failed unlink under /tmp.
#
#    ⇒ a permanently-red unit is the cry-wolf decay this repo retires
#      everywhere else (`gotcha.a-check-that-cries-wolf-gets-silenced`, m.13):
#      a check nobody trusts gets silenced, and takes its neighbours with it.
#
# ⚠️ OWED, and deliberately not taken here:
#      · the at-cause move is `TMUX_TMPDIR` — put the socket outside /tmp
#        entirely, so no sweep can reach it by construction. that retires BOTH
#        the exemption and this exit-status clause. it changes the duct's
#        socket path, which every client and the server must agree on, so it
#        wants a grove to prove and is too wide for this defect

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/find /tmp -xdev -mindepth 1 ! -type s -mtime +3 -delete
SuccessExitStatus=1

# bound what a deletion can ever reach
ProtectSystem=strict
ReadWritePaths=/tmp
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
NoNewPrivileges=yes
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE CAP_FOWNER
RestrictNamespaces=yes
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
LockPersonality=yes
